   "expected_text": "The sky appears blue because of a phenomenon called Rayleigh scattering. When sunlight enters our atmosphere, it interacts with the tiny molecules of air. Shorter wavelengths of light, like blue and violet, are scattered more strongly than longer wavelengths, such as red. While our eyes are more sensitive to blue, the scattered blue light dominates our view, especially during the day when the sun is high. As the sun lowers toward the horizon, the sky shifts to cream or red because the light has to travel through more of the atmosphere before reaching our eyes.<|im_end|>",
